To Raise: The number of experience points required to raise a score by one point to that number.
a Virtue which allows them to do so. A sensible division is to spend 120 experience points on Abilities and 120 on Arts.
Magi must have the following minimum Abilities: Parma Magica 1, Magic Theory 1, Latin 1. Characters with lower scores would not be admitted to the Order. A character without a Latin score of least 4 and an Artes Liberales score of at least 1 is unable to read the books of the Order. This will seriously weaken the character relative to other"
        REFERENCES {
            "Parma Magica" -> #tzLH0KGUBQ1bIuciN3CeqsPr ^"Parma Magica"
            "Magic Theory" -> #t7KPhjriPvQO5FqBFYxCbWYN ^"Magic Theory"
            "Artes Liberales" -> #trziwPnyEqQoiEWqQBqtGPCH ^"Artes Liberales"
        }
    }
    #tDEENxmJWyy0TyfhPB0gx2DX ^"Recommended Minimum Abilities" DEF {
        APPLIES TO [^"Magus"]
        DESCRIPTION "Artes Liberales 1 Latin 4 Magic Theory 3 Parma Magica 1 (should be no higher if the magus is just out of apprenticeship) Total Cost: 90 experience points
Do not forget that casting totals add a Technique and Form together, and that you will get a higher total by splitting experience between a Technique and a Form than by putting all the experience points in one or the other.
The highest level spell you can learn is equal to Technique + Form + Intelligence + Magic Theory +3, where the Technique and Form are the Technique and Form of the spell in question. If the spell has requisites (see page 311), they apply to this total as well. This is the appropriate Lab Total, assuming an aura modifier of +3, and thus any Virtues and Flaws your character has apply to this total if they would apply to a Lab Total in play."
        REFERENCES {
            "Artes Liberales" -> #trziwPnyEqQoiEWqQBqtGPCH ^"Artes Liberales"
            "Magic Theory" -> #t7KPhjriPvQO5FqBFYxCbWYN ^"Magic Theory"
            "Parma Magica" -> #tzLH0KGUBQ1bIuciN3CeqsPr ^"Parma Magica"
            "Magic Theory" -> #t7KPhjriPvQO5FqBFYxCbWYN ^"Magic Theory"
        }
    }
    #tDIjyFEKP95JSljPfqIL1KYh ^"After Apprenticeship" DEF {
        APPLIES TO [^"Magus"]
        PROPERTIES {
            ^"Points Per Year" INTEGER 30 FIXED
        }
        RULES {
            #to19TTG8jyoAsPGSHWMkJZCD: each_yearly_point_buys_one_art_or_ability_xp_or_one_spell_level
            #tXX4Iqn3n8F90oirHgb2R3LW: maximum_spell_level_limited_as_during_apprenticeship
        }
        DESCRIPTION "If you want to generate a magus who is some time out of apprenticeship, the rules change again. You might want to wait until you are familiar with the game system before doing this, however. There are a lot of options and considerations, which could easily seem overwhelming.
For every year, the magus gets 30 points. Each point can be an experience point in an Art or Ability or one level of spell. The maximum spell level a magus may know is limited as before. Thus, a magus who is 20 years out of apprenticeship could have a highest Art of 15, while a magus 120 years out of apprenticeship might have a highest Art of 40.
Of course, you may not want your experienced magus to have spent all his time studying Abilities, Arts and spells. If you wish to have your magus engage in other lab activities (such as creating items, enchanting familiars, and in particular creating
Longevity Rituals) you will need to know more about the circumstances in which he has been working. These are ultimately up to the storyguide, but a typical situation might be as follows:
• The magus' covenant (for Aging rolls) gives a Living Conditions modifier of +1 (see Aging, page 391).
• The magus' lab is of only average quality, giving a modifier to Lab Totals of 0.
• The covenant has a Magic aura of 3.
• The magus may use a maximum number of pawns per year equal to Magic Theory x 5, of the Arts required. It is not possible to accumulate pawns from year to year, because this number already represents vis the magus has saved while studying from books and inventing spells.
Statistics for familiars and apprentices should be agreed with the storyguide, if required.
For each season that your magus spends working on a lab project, the character loses 10 points from the yearly 30 experience points, to a minimum of 0 if three or four seasons are spent on lab work. Thus it is most cost effective to have the magus engage in a full year of lab work at a time. It is suggested that you advance your magus year by year, spending several on study, then taking years out to do lab work as the character's statistics reach appropriate levels."
        REFERENCES {
            "Magic Theory" -> #t7KPhjriPvQO5FqBFYxCbWYN ^"Magic Theory"
        }
    }
    #t4kE1CfJ98Y0vT7e3IIIXqRM ^"Personality at Creation" DEF {
        APPLIES TO [^"Character"]
        DESCRIPTION "Choose a few words to describe your character's personality, and attach a value between –3 and +3 to each. These are your character's Personality Traits (see page 28). If you have a Minor Personality Flaw, you should represent that by a Personality Trait with a score of +3 or –3, and a Major Personality Flaw should have a Personality Trait of +6 or –6.
Grogs should have a score (positive or negative) in Loyal, and warriors should have a score in Brave. Most warriors will have a positive score in Brave, but not all.
For companions and magi, they are normally nothing more than a guide to roleplaying, although you can roll them if you really want to, so you shouldn't worry too much about them. A few specific rules do require rolls on Personality Traits, however."
        REFERENCES {
            "Personality Trait" -> #tcBZnRedWJ5vEcmMkio8kULU ^"Personality Trait"
            "Personality Trait" -> #tcBZnRedWJ5vEcmMkio8kULU ^"Personality Trait"
        }
    }
    #t7nCyCXRdmZ32Ao7bUERBism ^"Reputations at Creation" DEF {
        APPLIES TO [^"Character"]
        DESCRIPTION "Characters only start with a Reputation if they choose a Virtue or Flaw that grants one, but all characters can develop them in play. See page 28 for rules on Reputations."
    }
    #txCEry46YvCwU5csHYwNEX4P ^"Confidence at Creation" DEF {
        APPLIES TO [^"Character"]
        DESCRIPTION "Grogs do not have Confidence Points. Like Story Flaws, Confidence Points indicate a central character.
Companions and Magi start with a Confidence Score of 1 and 3 Confidence Points, unless they have a Virtue or Flaw that indicates otherwise.
Confidence Points can be spent to gain a one-off +3 bonus to any die roll, and a maximum number of Confidence Points equal to the character's Confidence Score may be spent on a single roll. More details on Confidence can be found on page 29."
    }
    #tY7S06KijZMzmLJotsPKwfvt ^"Equipment at Creation" DEF {
        APPLIES TO [^"Character"]
        DESCRIPTION "Your character may start with any equipment or possessions she might reasonably have acquired and retained over her career. You need not list all her possessions."
    }
    #txgRdxBWVHkXKZ2xfhq7zmXb ^"Option: Extremely Complex Character Generation" DEF {
        APPLIES TO [^"Character"]
        DESCRIPTION "It is possible to generate a character by working out exactly what he has done in every season he has been alive, and using the rules in the Laboratory and Long-Term Events chapters to determine the results. This is how magi and important companions are normally advanced in play, but it takes a very long time for character generation. It tends to produce characters who are close to those generated using the detailed rules, but with a lot more character.
Even if you do generate a character this way, you should still use the normal guidelines for the first five years of life, and you assign Virtues, Flaws, and Characteristics in the normal way. However, age-based limits on Abilities do not apply, and all Virtues and Flaws have their normal effect on character advancement. You must discuss with your troupe the resources that the character has available; with sensible choices, it is usually hard to raise Abilities above the age-based limits, which is why they exist in Detailed Character Creation."
        REFERENCES {
            "Detailed Character Creation" -> #tQKyfk9YDoR30KmV9yeZZDo2 ^"Detailed Character Creation"
        }
    }
    # WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
    # 
    # ArMDef covers these concepts in its NARRATIVE files, and a .lore file
    # contributes only its own basename to the corpus entity set -- its internal
    # section headings never resolve as named entities. So a cross-reference to
    # ^"Mythic Europe" has nothing to land on, even though ArMDef devotes a
    # chapter to it. The re-targeted Fifth Edition sourcebooks make 88 such
    # references between them.
    # 
    # Each DEF here is an ANCHOR for a concept ArMDef already has, and its
    # DESCRIPTION is ArMDef's OWN opening definition of that concept, quoted from
    # ArMDef's own .lore. Nothing is taken from the sourcebooks, and nothing is
    # written fresh -- the owner's constraint, and the right one: the sourcebooks
    # are Fifth Edition products and their words do not belong in this edition.
    # 
    # These are deliberately thin. The chapter remains the real treatment; this is
    # the handle a reference can hold, mirroring the narrative rather than
    # duplicating it.
    # armdef-0.5-mythic-europe.lore :: "XIV. Mythic Europe" -- 60 references
    #tARMDefConceptMythicEurope ^"Mythic Europe" DEF {
        PROPERTIES {
            ^"Treated In" STRING "armdef-0.5-mythic-europe.lore"
            ^"Source" STRING DEFAULT "Core Rules"
        }
        DESCRIPTION "The official setting for **Ars ** **Magica** is Mythic Europe, a world very like the Middle Ages of our world, but with magic. For many people, this setting is a large part of the fun of the game. You can 'change history', or at least 'visit' historical places. When you save a country from demons, you've saved France, not some imaginary land. It's also possible to actually visit the place where your saga is set, although that's easier for European players."
    }
    # armdef-0.5-order-history.lore :: "II.The Order of Hermes" -- 28 references
    #tARMDefConceptOrderOfHermes ^"Order of Hermes" DEF {
        PROPERTIES {
            ^"Treated In" STRING "armdef-0.5-order-history.lore"
            ^"Source" STRING DEFAULT "Core Rules"
        }
        DESCRIPTION "The Order of Hermes is an organization of magi that spans the whole of Mythic Europe, even reaching some distance beyond. It has about 1200 members, living in thirteen Tribunals which divide up the continent. In the Magical Realm, no single group can even think to challenge the Order as a whole, but the power of God is far beyond even Hermetic magi. This chapter lays out the history and current state of the Order of Hermes, to which **Ars Magica** characters are almost all affiliated in some way."
    }
    # ── The owner's rulings, 2026-07-29 ────────────────────────────────────────
    # Six of the remaining concepts resolved to records ArMDef ALREADY declares
    # under another name, and those are re-pointed by the transformer rather than
    # redeclared here (^"The Divine" -> ^"The Divine Realm", ^"The Infernal" ->
    # ^"The Infernal Realm", ^"Aging Points" and ^"Aging Rolls" -> ^"Aging",
    # ^"Damage Table" -> the merged record). The rest are declared below.
    # A stub, at the owner's direction. ArMDef devotes a whole chapter to
    # Hermetic magic but declares no record for the concept itself, and its only
    # passage under that heading is a vignette rather than a definition -- so the
    # DESCRIPTION here is an editorial anchor note, NOT quoted rules text, and
    # says so. 18 references, from six of the nine sourcebooks.
    #tARMDefConceptHermeticMagic ^"Hermetic Magic" DEF {
        PROPERTIES {
            ^"Treated In" STRING "armdef-0.5-hermetic-magic.ttrpg"
            ^"Source" STRING DEFAULT "Core Rules"
        }
        DESCRIPTION "Anchor for the magic practised by the members of the Order of Hermes. ArMDef treats it in full in its Hermetic Magic chapter; this record exists so that cross-references to the concept resolve."
    }
    # ArMDef states the Lab Total inside ^"Basic Laboratory Activities", which is
    # scoped to a Laboratory rather than to the sum itself. The sum is referenced
    # on its own 12 times, so it gets its own record -- ArMDef's own words and
    # ArMDef's own formula, lifted from that record, not rewritten.
    #tARMDefConceptLabTotal ^"Lab Total" DEF {
        APPLIES TO [^"Magus"]
        PROPERTIES {
            ^"Source" STRING DEFAULT "Core Rules"
        }
        FORMULAS {
            "YOUR BASIC LAB TOTAL IS"
            "Technique + Form + Intelligence + Magic Theory + Aura Modifier"
        }
        DESCRIPTION "You use one sum, called the Lab Total, frequently in calculating your ability to accomplish various laboratory tasks."
    }
    # Separate from ^"Lab Texts (Build Points)", at the owner's direction: that
    # record is the COST of stocking a covenant's library, this one is the thing
    # itself. Build Points may be spent on Lab Texts; they are not the same record.
    #tARMDefConceptLabTexts ^"Lab Texts" DEF {
        PROPERTIES {
            ^"Source" STRING DEFAULT "Core Rules"
        }
        DESCRIPTION "Anchor for lab texts themselves -- the written record of a magus's laboratory work, which allows another magus to repeat it far more quickly. Their cost as a covenant's starting resource is the separate ^\"Lab Texts (Build Points)\" record."
        REFERENCES {
            "Lab Texts (Build Points)" -> ^"Lab Texts (Build Points)"
        }
    }
    # The paired Arts of a Hermetic spell. ArMDef declares ^"Hermetic Technique"
    # and ^"Hermetic Form" separately; the sourcebooks reference the pair as one
    # concept when naming what a spell is built from.
    #tARMDefConceptTechniqueForm ^"Technique and Form" DEF {
        APPLIES TO [^"Formulaic Spell"]
        PROPERTIES {
            ^"Source" STRING DEFAULT "Core Rules"
        }
        DESCRIPTION "Anchor for the paired Arts that define a Hermetic spell -- its Technique and its Form. ArMDef declares each separately as ^\"Hermetic Technique\" and ^\"Hermetic Art\"."
    }
    # armdef-0.5-mythic-europe.lore :: "Sacraments" -- ArMDef's own sentence.
    #tARMDefConceptExtremeUnction ^"Extreme Unction" DEF {
        PROPERTIES {
            ^"Treated In" STRING "armdef-0.5-mythic-europe.lore"
            ^"Source" STRING DEFAULT "Core Rules"
        }
        DESCRIPTION "Extreme Unction is the final sacrament, given just before death. For this reason, it draws the most ambiguous attitude of any sacrament. While it is generally believed to ease the passage to heaven, many people believe that, if you have received it, you are dead to the world in some sense, and must enter a monastery if you recover."
    }
    # From the printed ArMDef core table the owner supplied (pp.178-180 / p405).
    # ArMDef's own content, not the sourcebooks'.
    #tARMDefConceptWoundRecovery ^"Wound Recovery Table" DEF {
        APPLIES TO [^"Character"]
        PROPERTIES {
            ^"Table Columns" LIST ["Wound Level", "Interval", "Stable Ease Factor", "Improvement Ease Factor"]
            ^"Source" STRING DEFAULT "Core Rules"
        }
        FORMULAS {
            "RECOVERY TOTAL"
            "Stamina + Medic's Chirurgy or Medicine score + Magical Aid + Stress Die"
        }
        TABLE {
            "Light"
            "One week"
            "4"
            "10"
            "Medium"
            "One month"
            "6"
            "12"
            "Heavy"
            "One season"
            "9"
            "15"
            "Incapacitated"
            "See below"
            ""
            ""
        }
        DESCRIPTION "Roll interval determined by wound severity. Equal or Exceed Improvement Ease Factor: Wound improves by one level. Equal or Exceed Stable Ease Factor: Wound remains at same level, +3 bonus to future rolls. Below Stable Ease Factor: Wound becomes one level worse. Characters recover from all their wounds at the same time."
    }
    # Distinct from ^"Laboratory", at the owner's direction: the laboratory is the
    # place, this is a season's work done in one. ArMDef's ^"Basic Laboratory
    # Activities" enumerates them; this is the type they are instances of.
    #tARMDefConceptLabActivity ^"Laboratory Activity" DEF {
        APPLIES TO [^"Laboratory"]
        PROPERTIES {
            ^"Source" STRING DEFAULT "Core Rules"
        }
        DESCRIPTION "Anchor for an activity a magus performs using a laboratory, as distinct from the laboratory itself. ArMDef enumerates the basic set in ^\"Basic Laboratory Activities\"."
    }
    # EXTENDS ArMDef's own ^"Animal Companion", at the owner's direction.
    #tARMDefConceptMagicalAnimalComp ^"Magical Animal Companion" DEF {
        EXTENDS ^"Animal Companion"
        PROPERTIES {
            ^"Source" STRING DEFAULT "Core Rules"
        }
        DESCRIPTION "An animal companion that is itself a creature of the Magic Realm."
    }
    # The owner's read: this is new in Grogs rather than inherited from core. It is
    # declared here because ArMDef is what the re-targeted book now builds on, and
    # a reference has to land somewhere -- but WHERE it belongs is flagged in
    # ARMDEF-RETARGET-findings.md, because a Grogs-only rule arguably belongs in
    # the Grogs corpus, not in ArMDef core.
    # Owner's ruling, 2026-07-29: make it an extends. A Personality Roll is built
    # on a Personality Trait, so it EXTENDS ArMDef's ^"Personality Trait" rather
    # than standing as a bare ^"Character" anchor.
    #tARMDefConceptPersonalityRolls ^"Personality Rolls" DEF {
        EXTENDS ^"Personality Trait"
        PROPERTIES {
            ^"Source" STRING DEFAULT "Core Rules"
        }
        DESCRIPTION "A roll made against a Personality Trait to determine how a character behaves."
    }
    # A story genre ArMDef treats in armdef-0.5-stories.lore :: "Exploration".
    # ArMDef declares no story-genre records, so House Grogs' `MODIFY ^"Exploration"`
    # (which adds its own Grogs Story Genre note) had nothing to resolve against --
    # the one remaining validator error. Declared here from ArMDef's OWN lore text,
    # the same concept-anchor pattern as ^"Mythic Europe"; the Grogs MODIFY then
    # augments it. Not taken from the Grogs sourcebook.
    #tARMDefConceptExploration ^"Exploration" DEF {
        APPLIES TO [^"Character"]
        PROPERTIES {
            ^"Treated In" STRING "armdef-0.5-stories.lore"
            ^"Source" STRING DEFAULT "Core Rules"
            ^"Grogs Story Genre" LIST ["These stories feature new experiences or take place in exotic locales, and may be combined with action or with drama.", "Examples include:", "• A grog was forced to leave his father's sword in the body of a faerie on a recent adventure; he wants it back. However, the faerie has become something else now and the sword has become integral to that change. The grog and his comrades must succeed in the challenges posed by the faerie to regain the sword.", "• The grogs are shipwrecked on a mysterious island, and the magi are nowhere to be found. The secret to escaping from this regio must be somewhere on the island, but can they find it alone?", "• The grogs are required to deliver a magical item to the king of the monkeys. When they step across the borders of his land, they find themselves transformed into monkeys themselves. To complete their mission, they must foil a plot of the monkeys' enemies, the serpent people."]
            ^"Grogs Source" STRING "Grogs"
        }
        DESCRIPTION "The characters go somewhere they haven't been before, and find out about it. This covers going into caves, killing monsters, and taking their stuff, but it is much broader. It also includes traveling to the local abbey and trying to set up good relations with the abbot, finding a faerie forest and learning how to collect raw vis there, and even visiting other covenants of the Order for the first time."
    }
    # Base types for the chapter VI Covenants content
    # (armdef-0.5-covenants.ttrpg). The covenant is an ACTOR: "the single most
    # important character in most Ars Magica sagas". Hook/Boon entries are
    # Flaw/Virtue-shaped enumerables; printed names recur across kinds and
    # magnitudes, so instances carry disambiguated names ("Monster (Minor
    # Hook)") with the magnitude as a property.
    #tqPOcmupI8sEPkY2NKMNZUVO ACTOR "Covenant" DEF {
        PROPERTIES {
            ^"Season" STRING
            ^"Aura" INTEGER
            ^"Build Points" INTEGER
            ^"Hooks" LIST OF STRING
            ^"Boons" LIST OF STRING
            ^"Library" STRING
            ^"Lab Texts" STRING
            ^"Casting Tablets" STRING
            ^"Vis" STRING
            ^"Enchanted Items" STRING
            ^"Income Sources" LIST OF STRING
            ^"Prevailing Loyalty" INTEGER
            ^"Source" STRING DEFAULT "Core Rules"
        }
        DESCRIPTION "The covenant is the single most important character in most ARS MAGICA sagas, because it is the character that all the players contribute to. It is their home, a secure base, and the place where they study, improve, and create magical wonders. Thus, coming up with a concept for the covenant is very important, as it will have a strong influence on the feel of the saga. A saga based on a covenant located in a hidden regio in the heart of Constantinople will be very different from one based in a covenant occupying a large castle on an island off the coast of Scotland, and different again from one based in a covenant in a magical glade in the heart of a haunted forest in Brittany.
Although the covenant concept has an influence, it isn't all-defining. Many stories will, after all, take the magi away from their covenant, and if the troupe decides that it likes a particular sort of story, most of the stories may take place away from home. Covenants can even be moved, a proposition that will create a lot of stories in itself. This means that you should choose a concept you like when you start out, without worrying that you will be stuck with it forever."
    }
    #tQFcOosEBZ5B5Ze4p4Y1j7ly ^"Covenant Season" DEF {
        APPLIES TO [^"Covenant"]
        PROPERTIES {
            ^"Source" STRING DEFAULT "Core Rules"
        }
        DESCRIPTION "The Order of Hermes traditionally classifies covenants into four seasons: Spring, Summer, Autumn, and Winter. These informal labels refer to the different stages of a covenant's life, and most magi agree on them most of the time. They are also an important part of the covenant concept, because a lot of features depend on the season."
    }
    #tVLjZ6kwgeYw5FGnoxtkf0cd ^"Covenant Situation" DEF {
        APPLIES TO [^"Covenant"]
        PROPERTIES {
            ^"Hooks" LIST OF STRING
            ^"Boons" LIST OF STRING
            ^"Source" STRING DEFAULT "Core Rules"
        }
    }
    #tPhoL2rnJvbItWypGngx8n2N ^"Hook" DEF {
        APPLIES TO [^"Covenant"]
        PROPERTIES {
            ^"Magnitude" ENUM ["Minor", "Major"] REQUIRED
            ^"Requires" STRING
            ^"Source" STRING DEFAULT "Core Rules"
        }
    }
    #te6NaMwV2LTJOs2GI64z5WYp ^"Boon" DEF {
        APPLIES TO [^"Covenant"]
        PROPERTIES {
            ^"Magnitude" ENUM ["Minor", "Major"] REQUIRED
            ^"Requires" STRING
            ^"Source" STRING DEFAULT "Core Rules"
        }
    }
    #tJLCOrGO5QuneInU5WPRuRMx ^"Income Source" DEF {
        APPLIES TO [^"Covenant"]
        PROPERTIES {
            ^"Source" STRING DEFAULT "Covenants"
        }
        DESCRIPTION "A named way a covenant earns money each year (Agriculture, Trade, Wizardry,...), with its own pros, cons, and magical options."
    }
    #tZ5rXgVH6FkSaSXpwQ7lcruK ^"Covenfolk Category" DEF {
        APPLIES TO [^"Covenant"]
        PROPERTIES {
            ^"Source" STRING DEFAULT "Covenants"
        }
        DESCRIPTION "One of the accounting categories the covenfolk are separated into for the purposes of wages and provisioning (Craftsmen, Grogs, Servants,...)."
    }
    #tnbzNOdDkW9kLaRSpRlbvy5T ^"Expenditure Category" DEF {
        APPLIES TO [^"Covenant"]
        PROPERTIES {
            ^"Source" STRING DEFAULT "Covenants"
        }
        DESCRIPTION "One of the categories a covenant's regular yearly expenditure is divided into (Buildings, Provisions, Wages,...), each with its own cost rule."
    }
    #t8XCVIcYP0fTZE4WdgSGa3SO ^"Covenfolk Organization" DEF {
        APPLIES TO [^"Covenant"]
        PROPERTIES {
            ^"Source" STRING DEFAULT "Core Rules"
        }
    }
    # Minimal base type for Bestiary creatures (armdef-0.5-bestiary-*.ttrpg).
    # Individual creatures set concrete inline values; system sub-blocks
    # (PERSONALITY_TRAITS / COMBAT / ABILITIES / POWERS) carry the typed
    # lists. Mundane Beasts have no Might/Might Realm.
    #taDz3ITq9fRoE374AgiL8VE4 ^"Creature" DEF {
        PROPERTIES {
            ^"Might" INTEGER MIN 0
            ^"Might Realm" ENUM ["Magic", "Faerie", "Infernal", "Divine"]
            ^"Form" STRING
            ^"Size" INTEGER
            ^"Characteristics" DEF {
                ^"Intelligence" INTEGER
                ^"Cunning" INTEGER
                ^"Perception" INTEGER
                ^"Presence" INTEGER
                ^"Communication" INTEGER
                ^"Strength" INTEGER
                ^"Stamina" INTEGER
                ^"Dexterity" INTEGER
                ^"Quickness" INTEGER
            }
            ^"Virtues and Flaws" LIST OF STRING
            ^"Reputations" LIST OF STRING
            ^"Soak" INTEGER
            ^"Fatigue Levels" STRING
            ^"Wound Penalties" STRING
            ^"Vis" STRING
            ^"Appearance" STRING
            ^"Source" STRING DEFAULT "Core Rules"
        }
    }
    # Minimal base type for the twelve Hermetic Houses
    # (armdef-0.5-houses.ttrpg). Individual houses set concrete inline values.
    #tlabSpgxsPGTTR2WePb65NMD ^"Hermetic House" DEF {
        APPLIES TO [^"Magus"]
        PROPERTIES {
            ^"House Type" ENUM ["True Lineage", "Mystery Cult", "Societas"] REQUIRED
            ^"Prima" STRING
            ^"Domus Magna" STRING
            ^"Benefit" STRING
            ^"Outer Mystery" STRING
            ^"Source" STRING DEFAULT "Core Rules"
        }
    }
    # Base types for the chapter VIII Laboratory content
    # (armdef-0.5-laboratory.ttrpg). A laboratory is a personalizable entity
    # with eight Characteristics, Specializations, and its own Virtues/Flaws
    # (Structure/Outfittings/Supernatural x Major/Minor/Free). Shape and
    # Material entries carry the enchantment bonus list; their table is
    # parsed deterministically from the source (scrambled rows are marked
    # FIAT pending human review).
    #taKk3J80C97U9JM8e8grpEvf ^"Laboratory" DEF {
        PROPERTIES {
            ^"Size" INTEGER
            ^"Refinement" INTEGER
            ^"General Quality" INTEGER
            ^"Upkeep" INTEGER
            ^"Safety" INTEGER
            ^"Warping" INTEGER
            ^"Health" INTEGER
            ^"Aesthetics" INTEGER
            ^"Source" STRING DEFAULT "Covenants"
        }
        DESCRIPTION "A magus's laboratory, described in its own right: a set of numeric characteristics plus any number of Laboratory Virtues and Flaws."
        REFERENCES {
            "Virtues and Flaws" -> ^"Virtues and Flaws"
        }
    }
    #tdU9fWu0umjjKOg8i8JPY8aw ^"Laboratory Characteristic" DEF {
        APPLIES TO [^"Laboratory"]
        PROPERTIES {
            ^"Source" STRING DEFAULT "Core Rules"
        }
    }
